    "summary": "High‑energy Santa Fe ’82 show with fire Throwing Stones & monster after‑drums",
    "review": "The October 17, 1982 gig at Santa Fe Downs is a pure burst of late‑‘80 energy. From the opening “Mississippi Half‑Step” through a ripping first set, the band never loses its spark, and the second set explodes into a six‑song after‑drums marathon that keeps the crowd soaring. Highlights include a smoking “Throwing Stones,” a thunderous “The Other One” where Phil’s bass roars, an inspired “Althea,” and a post‑drums ballad from Jerry that many fans still quote. Brent even slipped in a special Santa Fe verse on “Never Trust A Woman,” adding a unique local flavor. The audience tape captures the ambience beautifully—clear, well‑balanced sound with just a few minor glitches—making it the go‑to source for this fire‑filled night.\n\nIf you’re hunting a 1982 snapshot of the Dead at full tilt, this show delivers everything: tight rhythm work from Bob and the twins on drums, Brent’s tasteful keys, and Jerry’s signature vocal magic. The flow from “Estimated Prophet” straight into “He’s Gone,” then through an extended “Space/Throwing Stones/Goin’ Down The Road Feeling Bad/Wheel/The Other One/Wharf Rat/Good Lovin'” sequence is seamless and keeps the momentum high all night long.",
